Q4. Preparing to merge

git switch main (or git checkout main) — switches to the target branch (main).

git status — checks that your working directory is clean with no uncommitted changes before merging.

Q5. Understanding merge types

What type of merge will happen: A three-way merge (because both branches have diverged with new commits).

What will Git create: A new merge commit that ties both branch histories together.

Hard
Q6. The merge scenario

What fast-forward means: Git simply moved the main branch pointer forward to match the latest commit on the feature branch without creating an extra merge commit.

Why this was possible: main had no new commits added to it after the feature branch was created, so there was a direct linear path between them.

What would happen if main had changed: Git would perform a three-way merge instead and create a new merge commit (or ask you to resolve merge conflicts if the same lines were edited).

Q7. Build a merge workflow

